# {color:#36b37e}What would be the period the couriers are blocked for this issue (days, months, years or hours) It’s necessary to have it as short as possible as the block for years or even months can be considered an automated decision-making with legal effect (couriers will not be able to make money for living within a substantial period of time){color}
## The suspension would be an “investigative” one - i.e. system detects cash fraud, courier is temporarily suspended and then the data regarding the suspension goes to a CS agent, who can then review and extend (or not) the suspension. The length of the initial “investigative” suspension is up for discussion - what would be the appropriate length that it could still argued that it does not have a significant legal effect? Would 48 hours work? 
# {color:#36b37e}Do we have an RFC on how exactly the automation will work? It’s hard to tell whether this is ok or not as we have to ensure that the algorithm for the blocking is well-defined and there is a way for couriers to dispute this block {color}
## [+Fraud Checks by CS+|https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1dpGeNItwkBwQjuHcPDxKCw76xmoJ6UUl8qS7y2BbwFA/edit?gid=2010090126#gid=2010090126]  - column G shows the logic behind the fraud checks. 
# {color:#36b37e}Did the team check the system for biases or false positive blocks?{color}
## With fraud, it can never be argued for certain that the courier’s intention or a weird edge case. However, the couriers are given a lot of benefit of doubt, so if they are flagged as fraudulent, it is common sense/can be reasonably assumed that the courier is fraudulent. The criteria is established with sufficient “leeway” so that realistically, it would be hard to believe that couriers meeting the criteria are not committing fraud. 
# {color:#36b37e}Will the team use any internally developed solution for triggering such blocks (like Beehive rules) or will the team leverage other service providers for this?   {color}
## Internally, the fraud engine built by Bolt, based on the rules [+Fraud Checks by CS+|https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1dpGeNItwkBwQjuHcPDxKCw76xmoJ6UUl8qS7y2BbwFA/edit?gid=2010090126#gid=2010090126]  - column G shows the logic behind the fraud checks. 
# {color:#36b37e}What information will be taken into account for blocking a courier?{color}
## [+Fraud Checks by CS+|https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1dpGeNItwkBwQjuHcPDxKCw76xmoJ6UUl8qS7y2BbwFA/edit?gid=2010090126#gid=2010090126]  - column G shows the logic behind the fraud checks. 
# {color:#36b37e}Would the previous CS tickets or courier’s reports be taken into account before blocking?{color}
## No, not for the initial automatic investigative block duration. 
# {color:#36b37e}Will we block the courier after the first time they falsely reported this case or would there be a warning before?{color}
## No warning for fraud investigative blocks.

